the washing post calls author jody pico  the queen of ethical dilemma fiction  that's a title in her book she tackles  controversial and tough topics from race  relations to the holocaust and school  shootings  her last nine books debuted at number  one on the new york times bestsellers  list  pico's new novel a spark of light takes  on women's reproductive rights  now the book follows a diverse group of  people over the course of one day during  a deadly shooting at a mississippi  abortion clinic  jody pico joins us at the table to  discuss hey jody how are you  so let me tell you this this is what usa  today says about this book a spark of  light  is an uncomfortable disturbing read  abortion is so  highly charged and everybody has very  strong emotions on both sides  right why did you decide that you wanted  to write about this topic  so when i was in college i had a very  good friend who was seven weeks pregnant  unwanted pregnancy and wound up deciding  to have an abortion  which i completely supported years later  i was pregnant with my third child  i too was seven weeks pregnant and i had  a complication and i was told i might  lose this baby  and it was a baby to me and i couldn't  figure out  how i could hold both of those concepts  in my mind at the same time  in america we tend to legislate  reproductive rights  but laws are black and white and the  lives of women are a thousand shades of  grey  and that was why i wanted to write this  book did you want it to be disturbing  you talked to people on both sides i  absolutely did you know this is not an  issue that we are ever going to agree on  and one of the things that i learned  speaking to people who think differently  than me  is that they come from a place of deep  conviction and deep compassion as well  they believe  what they believe and we have to find a  way to work together in spite of that  um what i want is to not tell you what  to think in my book and i  i think if you read the spark of light  i'm not going to tell you there's a  right answer  what i am going to do is make sure that  you hear from the other side and i know  you spoke with dozens and dozens of  people  um did it change your views at all um it  certainly  enlightened me you know i could find  myself having things in common with  people who were pro-life because i  identify as pro-choice  and understanding why they they feel the  way that they do  it didn't change how i feel about  reproductive rights and what i think  women  need uh but the thing that really  surprised me the most and that made me  quite sad  was that i interviewed 151 women who had  terminated a pregnancy of those 151  one woman regretted it all of them  thought about it daily  of those 151 less than 25 were willing  to be acknowledged in the book  and they're living under this umbrella  of stigma and shame  because when women don't speak up people  fill in the story for them  and the narratives are often one of  blame on these kinds of stories of  abortion in particular people cling to  certainty they want  in their opinions and uncertainty makes  people itchy  you have to live in the uncertainty you  have to have it be your whole life  what is that like and does that get  irritating no i  love that actually and and one of the  reasons i was drawn to this topic  is because the uncertainty exists for an  individual woman over the course of her  own life  what you believe at 14 is not what you  think at 30 or at 45.  you know and that's why again i keep  coming back to the fact that  we need to move past legislating  reproductive rights we have to take roe  versus wade off the table leave it alone  and begin to come up with solutions that  that move beyond that  and it's just that i mean nora mentioned  how much research you put into this book  what takeaway from all the researchers  and experts that you talked to  gave you a feeling that maybe this  doesn't have to be such a polarizing  issue  well i think it's in everyone's best  interest to lower the abortion rate  because nobody goes in  wanting to have an abortion right that's  a last resort  and it is its lowest in decades it is it  is although that's kind of a weird  statistic because it's still  rising for disadvantaged women and women  of color socioeconomic exactly  and 75 of women who have an abortion say  it's an economic issue  i think that what we need to do is to  open up conversations about  contraception  as we just heard chelsea clinton say  there are ways to teach sex ed in school  that are age-appropriate  having free and available contraception  lowers the abortion rate  and even spark of light is related to  this issue  right even even the title through me  yeah so that comes from a medical  journal it's a real thing  they are able to see the moment when a  sperm fertilizes an egg under a very  high-powered microscope and when that  happens there is a spark of light that's  released that is zinc  actually and what's cool about it is  that it's something that can be used in  in vitro  the healthiest embryos are the ones with  the biggest sparks well the cover of the  book would be a sparkle light as well  now i mentioned how beautiful it is  and it really is beautiful jody pico  thank you thank you for having me back  really appreciate it another best seller  for you  another one thank you i know so well  spark of light is out  now wherever you like to buy books
 

